Task: Aseprite adapter and integration test (Phase 3)

Red phase: the integration test was written first and failed on the missing
module. Green phase: twenty-six checks pass inside the real application.

The suite was then proved to be worth something. A deliberate defect was
introduced - cels placed at the original position instead of the offset one -
and the suite failed immediately, naming the frame, the expected value and the
actual one, and the process exited non-zero. The defect was reverted and the
suite went green again. Without that step, a passing integration test only
proves the code ran.

What the test pins down:
- The sprite grows by exactly one frame per step.
- The motion lands on a new layer; the original layer keeps its name and its
  cel keeps its position, which is the non-destructive guarantee stated as a
  quality gate.
- Every produced cel sits at the offset the path asked for, measured from where
  the original drawing was.
- Durations survive the conversion between the milliseconds the core speaks and
  the seconds the application stores.
- A tag covers exactly the produced range, so the result can be played back.

Findings worth keeping:
- An error raised inside a batch script makes the application exit with a
  non-zero status, which is what lets these tests mean something to a machine.
- The first runner reported zero checks while passing, because each test file
  loaded its own copy of the assertion helper. The runner now owns the one
  instance and passes it in, so the reported count is the count that ran.
